CH6 = [
    ("chapter", 6, "CONCLUSION AND FUTURE SCOPE"),
    ("h2", "6.1 Summary of Completed Contributions"),
    ("p", "The project delivers a working system that detects the instruments present in a recording and returns the stems the user selects while suppressing the others. The detector is trained for six classes and reaches a macro-F1 of 0.798 across them, and guitar and piano presence is estimated from the pretrained backend. A single query-conditioned U-Net with 8.8 million parameters, trained on MUSDB18-HQ and Saraga, extracts violin, mridangam and ghatam masks, and a competitive mask combines them with a pretrained 6-source model for the Western stems. A web application with audio and video input exposes the pipeline."),
    ("p", "The measured outcomes are these. Best validation SI-SDR rose from −8.48 dB in the first repaired-validation configuration to +2.53 dB in the final configuration, and the largest step came from repairing the data. On 12 Carnatic validation tracks the trained model beat the best pretrained output by 0.8 dB for vocals, 2.8 dB for violin and 4.4 dB for mridangam. The competitive mask raised leakage suppression to 28.2 dB SIR for vocals and 27.6 dB for drums on four test tracks. The competitive mask substantially reduces leakage from unselected instruments, although residual interference remains, and requests for absent violin or mridangam still return audible output. Leakage was the practical problem that started the work, and it is reduced and not solved."),
    ("h2", "6.2 Key Theoretical and Practical Takeaways"),
    ("p", "Three lessons stand out. First, in a task with scarce and noisy ground truth, auditing the data gave more than changing the network. The faults were found with a simple diagnostic that scores the raw mixture and the ideal mask next to the model. Second, a mask model should be trained on requests it should refuse. Absent-query examples were added because the separator otherwise answered every request with sound, and they reduce that behaviour without removing it for violin and mridangam. Third, leakage is a property of how masks are combined, and sharing each bin among all sources was more effective than making each mask more accurate."),
    ("h2", "6.3 Limitations"),
    ("p", "The limits are listed here so that the results can be read correctly. Ghatam is a supported query class, but the model returns silence for it, because only 46 training recordings contain it. Violin separation is about 1 dB on mixed recordings. Saraga stems contain bleed, so the Carnatic scores are measured against imperfect ground truth. Carnatic training and validation use summed stems, and the quality on real concert mixes is unmeasured. The validation sets hold 11 to 30 clips per stem. No listening test was run. Guitar and piano cannot be trained or scored separately because MUSDB provides one \"other\" stem for both, so their outputs are approximate melodic extractions. The U-Net does not model frequencies above about 11 kHz. The five Western stems rely on a pretrained model that was not trained in this project."),
    ("h2", "6.4 Future Research Directions"),
    ("p", "Future work follows the limits above. The first direction is better Carnatic data: the studio-quality Sanidha recordings [11] for clean training and evaluation, and more ghatam and violin recordings. The second is real labels for guitar and piano from multitrack collections that name the two instruments separately, which would also allow an independent held-out test set. The third is evaluation: a listening test, per-clip distributions as recommended in [20], and perceptual measures such as MAPSS [19]. The fourth is the model itself, with a complex-valued or waveform-domain separator to remove the phase ceiling and fine-tuning on real concert mixes. The fifth is richer queries, such as groups of instruments [2], and a detector built on a frozen audio foundation model [13]."),
]

